{
  "title": "Port the server to SQLite and cut prod over (step 2 of the SQLite pilot)",
  "description": "doc/sqlite-pilot-analysis.md is the step-1 pre-analysis: it lists every Postgres-specific feature with its SQLite equivalent, has benchmarks from scripts/sqlite-pilot-bench.py, and recommends going ahead as a cutover rather than keeping two backends permanently. \
If the owner says go: add a SQLite backend (pool with WAL/busy_timeout/foreign_keys pragmas and app-level write serialization, a baseline SQLite migration, and uuid/JSON column newtypes); port the search filter to an FTS5 trigram index over current versions with an unaccent UDF fallback for tokens under 3 characters; replace the pg_current_xact_id sync cursors with a sync_clock counter (coordinate with blocked-one-sync-mechanism; this also fixes p3-sync-cursor-after-restore); move recipe_versions.photo_ids off uuid[]; and replace citext tag names with a normalized key column. \
Run the e2e suite against both backends in CI until SQLite passes, write and rehearse the data migration from the doc's outline (keep soft-deleted rows, seed sync_clock above every migrated change_xid), cut prod over, then delete the Postgres backend.",
